1
00:00:00,059 --> 00:00:01,759
Before you make any meaningful change

2
00:00:01,759 --> 00:00:03,419
to your production model, you create

3
00:00:03,419 --> 00:00:05,719
a named saved version of your

4
00:00:05,719 --> 00:00:06,979
current ontology state.

5
00:00:07,559 --> 00:00:08,999
Version history allows you to restore

6
00:00:08,999 --> 00:00:10,679
that saved definition if things go

7
00:00:10,679 --> 00:00:12,759
sideways. So it's not a surgical

8
00:00:12,759 --> 00:00:14,339
rollback where I just undo the

9
00:00:14,339 --> 00:00:15,379
last five minutes of work?

10
00:00:15,439 --> 00:00:16,979
No, and that's a crucial limitation

11
00:00:16,979 --> 00:00:19,459
to understand. A restore replaces the

12
00:00:19,459 --> 00:00:21,759
definition as a whole, it overwrites

13
00:00:21,759 --> 00:00:23,159
everything currently in the system

14
00:00:23,159 --> 00:00:24,919
with the exact state of the

15
00:00:24,919 --> 00:00:26,859
past version. So if your team

16
00:00:26,859 --> 00:00:28,459
spent four hours making great updates,

17
00:00:28,459 --> 00:00:30,299
and then someone makes one catastrophic

18
00:00:30,299 --> 00:00:32,679
mistake in hour five, rolling back

19
00:00:32,679 --> 00:00:34,239
to yesterday's version wipes out all

20
00:00:34,239 --> 00:00:35,599
the good work from those first

21
00:00:35,599 --> 00:00:36,779
four hours too.

22
00:00:36,879 --> 00:00:38,779
That's brutal. Best practice dictates

23
00:00:38,779 --> 00:00:40,039
that you must save a version

24
00:00:40,039 --> 00:00:42,079
of your current broken state before

25
00:00:42,079 --> 00:00:43,659
you restore the old one, just

26
00:00:43,659 --> 00:00:44,559
in case you need to salvage

27
00:00:44,559 --> 00:00:45,759
a piece of the new logic

28
00:00:45,759 --> 00:00:48,139
later. It's an effective safety net,

29
00:00:48,139 --> 00:00:50,159
but it requires really disciplined

30
00:00:50,159 --> 00:00:51,379
version control habits.
